  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nMoisture is well in place across the Borderland with a nice\nmoisture plume noticeable on WV extending well south into Mexico.\nCurrently the deepest moisture is over eastern AZ into western NM\nbut as upper trough associated with upper low over the Pacific NW\nshifts eastward, so will the moisture plume. A series of\ndisturbances moving north out of Mexico will interact with the \nmoisture and bring scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms\nto the region. Through Thursday, best rain chances are west of \nthe RGV but as we get into Thu night and into Sat, the focus \nshifts to the east. PW's of 1.3-1.5\" will make storms very \nefficient with locally heavy rainfall possible and the wind flow \nout of the south could lead to some training. Extended the Flood \nWatch in the Gila to go through 12Z Thu and issued one for the \nlowlands west of the RGV as well as the Sacs for 18Z Wed-12Z Thu. \nExpect this will have to be pushed east later in the week. There \nwill be some increased wind shear as the trough approaches which \ncould lead to a few stronger/low end severe out west Wed/Thu. \nPreferred NBM PoPs over MAV/MET PoPs further east in the next \ncouple of days with so much moisture around, it won't take much to\ntrigger a storm.\n\nAs the upper trough pushes east during the day Sat, drier air\nquickly moves in. EC is in line with the GFS as far as this\ntiming, so little to no rain chances as we head into Sunday/Mon.\nMaybe another moisture surge after that, but still a lot of\ninconsistency in models with regards to handling of next trough\nover the southwest.",
